[What is a preference center?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/preference-center): A preference center is a page on a brand's app or website where customers control which messages they get (across email, push, SMS, and other channels) instead of facing a single all-or-nothing unsubscribe choice. It lets users tell a brand exactly what channels, topics, and frequency they're willing to receive, which keeps more of them reachable over time.

[What should a preference center include?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/preference-center): A preference center should include: Channel choices – options to opt in/out of email, push, SMS, in-app messages, etc. separately Topic or content categories – e.g., promotions vs. product updates vs. account/service notices (in Braze, these map to Subscription Groups) Frequency controls – how often a user wants to hear from you Simplicity – the article specifically warns against overwhelming users with too many choices; a handful of clear options works better than an exhaustive list Easy access, not just onboarding – it should be revisitable any time, not a one-time setup step, since periodic check-ins build trust A branded, on-platform look – it's worth customizing the design so it feels like a natural part of your product rather than a bare settings form Genuine follow-through – whatever preferences a user sets need to actually be respected in your sending logic, or the whole exercise is pointless

[What is an email preference center vs. an unsubscribe page?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/preference-center): An unsubscribe page is binary: The user leaves your list entirely, and you lose the ability to message them at all. An email preference center gives granular control instead: rather than turning email off completely, a user can narrow down to just the newsletters or categories they care about (e.g., deals but not product announcements). The brand keeps a communication channel open at a lower volume rather than losing it altogether.

[How preference centers reduce churn?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/preference-center): By letting users self-select relevant content and frequency from the start, brands avoid the fatigue that drives full opt-outs, protect deliverability, and end up with a smaller but more engaged and durable list of reachable customers, rather than risking losing users altogether.

[What is BrazeAI™?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-ai-in-action-launch): BrazeAI™ is a composable suite of generative, predictive, and agentic capabilities, that empower marketers to gain context, apply intelligence, and deliver more relevant interactions at scale.

[What is the difference between the BrazeAI Operator™ and the BrazeAI Agent Console™?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-ai-in-action-launch): The Operator provides instant guides on how to work with Braze and get the most out of every campaign built within the platform. It also works with the BrazeAI Agent Consoleᵀᴹ to build agents that can automate many tasks that used to require human oversight, such as sentiment analysis, dynamic segmentation, or responding to customer feedback.

[What results are brands seeing with BrazeAI™ agents?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-ai-in-action-launch): Luxury Escapes replaced manual segmentation with an agent capable of classifying users by level of intent, boosting revenue per user by 10% Dayuse leveraged the Agent Console to personalize the messages sent to customers based on each individual's purchase history, increasing repeat bookings by 23% ConsumerAffairs used the Agent Console to make recommendations to customers who weren't engaged or hadn't shared much personal data, boosting clicks per open by 3.5X and raising conversions by 2X.

[How does Aeroflow Health use AI agents with Braze?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-agents-customer-relationships): Aeroflow uses Braze for an SMS “re-ordering” journey—customers who are ready for a repurchase receive an SMS asking if they’d like to reorder in the same size.

[What results did Aeroflow Health see from AI agents?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-agents-customer-relationships): For Aeroflow, the results have been remarkable—a 8.8% lift in repurchase conversions over business as usual.

[What is the BrazeAI Agent Console™?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-agents-customer-relationships): It's a tool that lets marketers create customizable AI agents to perform business tasks, foster creativity, and act as strategic conductors of their engagement strategy—launched in April 2026 to help enrich data, personalize experiences, and better understand customers.

[What is the BrazeAI Operator™?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/brazeai-operator-product-spotlight): The BrazeAI Operatorᵀᴹ is your full-time assistant. Fully trained on Braze’s functionality, simply ask the Operator a question, give it a task, or even ask it to build an agent and see how quickly and accurately it responds.

[What can the BrazeAI Operator™ build in Braze?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/brazeai-operator-product-spotlight): The BrazeAI Operatorᵀᴹ is your full-time assistant. Fully trained on Braze’s functionality, simply ask the Operator a question, give it a task, or even ask it to build an agent and see how quickly and accurately it responds. The Operator is designed to build a wide range of content—such as emails, in-app messages, push notifications, and more—for cross-channel campaigns

[How does the Operator work with the BrazeAI Agent Console™?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/brazeai-operator-product-spotlight): The Operator provides instant guides on how to work with Braze and get the most out of every campaign built within the platform. It also works with the BrazeAI Agent Consoleᵀᴹ to build agents that can automate many tasks that used to require human oversight, such as sentiment analysis, dynamic segmentation, or responding to customer feedback.

[How can I reduce churn?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/churn-prevention): Reduce churn by spotting at-risk customers early and intervening with timely, personalized outreach. Track engagement drop-offs and usage decline, segment at-risk users, trigger real-time re-engagement and win-back across channels, deliver value early through onboarding, and keep testing interventions. Prevention works best proactively, across the whole lifecycle, rather than as a last-minute discount.

[What is churn prevention?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/churn-prevention): Churn prevention is the practice of identifying customers at risk of leaving and taking targeted, proactive steps to keep them. It relies on early behavioral signals and timely, personalized engagement, rather than reactive win-back campaigns after a customer has already decided to go.

[What is the difference between churn prevention and churn prediction?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/churn-prevention): Churn prediction uses data and machine learning to forecast which customers are likely to leave. Churn prevention is what you do about it, covering the strategies and interventions that keep at-risk customers engaged. Prediction supplies the signal, and prevention turns that signal into action.

[What is the difference between voluntary and involuntary churn?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/churn-prevention): Voluntary churn is when a customer actively chooses to leave, such as canceling a subscription. Involuntary churn happens passively, usually from failed payments or expired cards. Voluntary churn calls for engagement and value, while involuntary churn is often fixed with payment retries and reminders.

[How does personalization reduce churn?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/churn-prevention): Personalization reduces churn by making outreach feel relevant and considered. Tailored content, offers, and timing based on each customer's behavior and stage help people feel understood, so re-engagement reads as helpful rather than intrusive, which makes at-risk customers more likely to stay.

[What is an SMS marketing strategy, and why is it important for retention and conversions?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/a-complete-guide-to-sms-marketing): An SMS marketing strategy is a planned approach to reaching opted-in customers via text message with timely, relevant content. It matters for retention and conversions because SMS is a direct, personal channel that garners high open rates, making it one of the most effective ways to re-engage customers and drive purchases at the moments that count.

[How can brands build and maintain an SMS subscriber list effectively?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/a-complete-guide-to-sms-marketing): Brands can build an SMS subscriber list effectively by collecting phone numbers at high-intent moments and offering a clear incentive for subscribing. Maintaining the list means regularly removing invalid and deactivated numbers, honoring opt-outs right away, and continually adding new subscribers who are likely to engage on the channel.

[How does SMS complement email, push, in-app, and web messaging in a cross-channel strategy?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/a-complete-guide-to-sms-marketing): SMS complements a broader cross-channel strategy by handling the moments that need maximum immediacy and directness, such as time-sensitive offers, transactional updates, and high-priority alerts. Email works for longer-form communication, push for in-app users, and in-app messages for contextual nudges, while SMS reaches customers regardless of whether they have the app open. SMS pairs well with email: email for broader reach to your entire customer base and SMS for more targeted outreach to your high-value customers.

[What are best practices for segmenting and personalizing SMS campaigns?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/a-complete-guide-to-sms-marketing): Best practices for segmenting and personalizing SMS campaigns include segmenting your SMS subscriber list by behavior, purchase history, and lifecycle stage, then using dynamic content to make each message specific to the user. AI send-time optimization personalizes delivery further by identifying when each subscriber is most likely to engage, rather than applying a single send time to the whole list.

[How can brands measure the impact of SMS marketing campaigns on engagement and revenue?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/a-complete-guide-to-sms-marketing): Brands can measure the impact of SMS marketing campaigns by tracking delivery rates, click-through rates, conversion rates, and opt-out rates at the campaign level, then connecting those engagement metrics to broader business outcomes like retention and lifetime value. Cohort analysis comparing SMS-subscribed customers to non-subscribed customers reveals the channel's long-term contribution to revenue.

[Why should brands adopt WhatsApp for marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/the-powerful-impact-of-using-whatsapp-for-customer-engagement): Brands should adopt WhatsApp for marketing because it offers direct, personalized, and highly engaging communication with customers on a widely used platform.

[How does WhatsApp compare to other marketing channels?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/the-powerful-impact-of-using-whatsapp-for-customer-engagement): WhatsApp stands out from other marketing channels by enabling conversational interactions with high open and response rates.

[What benefits does WhatsApp provide as part of a cross-channel strategy?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/the-powerful-impact-of-using-whatsapp-for-customer-engagement): WhatsApp enhances a cross-channel strategy by providing seamless, instant customer engagement that complements email, SMS, and social media touchpoints.

[How can rich media and two-way messaging improve performance?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/the-powerful-impact-of-using-whatsapp-for-customer-engagement): Rich media and two-way messaging improve performance by making interactions more interactive, visually appealing, and fostering meaningful customer conversations.

[What is WhatsApp marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/whatsapp-marketing): WhatsApp marketing is the practice of reaching customers with personalized, interactive, two-way messages on the WhatsApp Business Platform. Brands use it for promotions, order updates, cart recovery, and support, integrated into cross-channel campaigns, to drive engagement, conversions, and loyalty on a channel billions of people use daily.

[How do I measure WhatsApp marketing success?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/whatsapp-marketing): Measure campaign performance with read rate, click-through rate, and conversion rate. Measure the program overall with uplift in sessions, purchases, retention and lifetime value versus other channels. Because WhatsApp is a premium channel, track incremental impact, not just raw sends.

[How do I set up a WhatsApp Business Account for marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/whatsapp-marketing): Connect WhatsApp to a customer engagement platform (your Business Solution Provider), create your Meta and WhatsApp Business accounts, verify your business, apply for Official Business Account status, scale your messaging limits, collect opt-ins, and submit message templates for approval before sending campaigns.

[Do you need opt-in for WhatsApp marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/whatsapp-marketing): Yes. WhatsApp requires explicit consent before sending marketing messages. Collect opt-ins through your website, landing pages, phone-capture forms, QR codes, and social links, be transparent about what subscribers will receive, and follow regional privacy laws to keep a high-quality sending reputation.

[What are the best WhatsApp marketing use cases?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/whatsapp-marketing): High-impact use cases include onboarding and abandoned-registration nudges, abandoned cart recovery, order updates, back-in-stock alerts, loyalty enrollment, upsell and cross-sell, surveys, and win-back campaigns. The strongest programs pair these with other channels and personalize with first-party data.

[What is conversational marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/conversational-marketing): Conversational marketing is a customer-centric approach built on real-time, one-to-one, two-way conversations across channels like chat, messaging apps (WhatsApp, Messenger), SMS, and email. Instead of broadcasting a single message, it engages each customer individually with personalized, context-aware dialogue, increasingly powered by AI so it can happen at scale.

[How is conversational marketing different from traditional marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/conversational-marketing): Traditional marketing broadcasts one message to a broad audience. Conversational marketing engages customers individually in real-time, two-way dialogue on their preferred channel, carrying context between interactions. The result feels personal and responsive rather than one-directional, which drives higher engagement and faster paths to purchase.

[What channels are used in conversational marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/conversational-marketing): Common channels include website live chat and chatbots, messaging apps like WhatsApp and Facebook Messenger, SMS, and increasingly email that supports replies. Strong programs coordinate these channels so a conversation can move between them without the customer having to repeat themselves.

[What role do AI agents play in conversational marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/conversational-marketing): AI agents let conversational marketing scale by responding in real time, personalizing replies, and carrying customer context across channels and journey stages. They handle common interactions and hand off to humans for complex or sensitive issues, so brands deliver two-way engagement without one-to-one manual effort.

[What are examples of conversational marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/conversational-marketing): Examples include a chatbot recommending products and answering FAQs, a click-to-message ad opening a WhatsApp conversation, personalized SMS order updates that invite replies, and a quiz-style bot that tailors content. The common thread is real-time, personalized, two-way interaction on the customer's channel of choice.

[What is customer acquisition?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Customer acquisition is the process of attracting and converting potential customers into paying buyers of a company's products or services. It spans every stage from first awareness to purchase, drawing on marketing, sales, and service. A customer acquisition strategy makes that process structured and repeatable rather than reliant on one-off campaigns.

[Why is customer acquisition important?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Customer acquisition is important because it drives the revenue that funds growth, sustains day-to-day operations, and offsets the customers every business loses over time. A steady flow of new customers also signals momentum to investors and partners, making consistent acquisition a mark of a business with staying power.

[How do awareness, consideration, and conversion contribute to customer acquisition?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Each stage moves a prospect closer to becoming a customer. In awareness, they discover your brand; in consideration, they weigh it against alternatives; in conversion, they decide to buy. A strong strategy meets prospects at every stage with relevant messaging, guiding them forward without pushing for the sale too early.

[How can sales and marketing teams collaborate for better customer acquisition?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Sales and marketing collaborate best when they share one definition of a high-value customer and align on messaging and targeting. That alignment gives prospects a consistent journey from first touch to purchase, cuts wasted effort on poor-fit leads, and typically lifts acquisition rates by focusing both teams on the same goals.

[What is the significance of brand awareness in customer acquisition?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Brand awareness sits at the top of the acquisition funnel and shapes everything after it. When people already recognize and trust a brand, they're far more likely to consider it and choose it over alternatives at the conversion stage. Strong awareness makes every later step of acquisition more efficient.

[What are some customer acquisition tools businesses should consider?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/customer-acquisition-strategy): Useful customer acquisition tools include CRM systems, email and cross-channel messaging platforms, social media management tools, and analytics software to track and optimize performance. A customer engagement platform like Braze brings channels, first-party data, and journey orchestration together in one place, so acquisition and retention run from a single view of each customer.

[What is generative AI for marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/generative-ai-for-marketing): Generative AI for marketing is the use of AI models that create new content, text, images, video, or code, from a prompt to support marketing work. Marketers use it to draft copy, produce creative variants, summarize data, and personalize messaging, with humans guiding strategy, accuracy, and brand voice.

[How does generative AI work in marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/generative-ai-for-marketing): Generative AI models learn patterns from large datasets and produce new outputs in response to prompts. In marketing, teams feed brand guidelines, past assets, and customer data, then prompt the model to generate on-brand copy, visuals, or drafts, often layering proprietary data onto a foundation model for relevance.

[What are the main use cases for generative AI in marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/generative-ai-for-marketing): Common use cases include content and creative generation, personalization and micro-segmentation, chatbots and customer experience, data analysis and predictive insight, process automation, and idea generation. The highest value comes when generated content connects to how it is selected and delivered to each customer.

[What is the difference between generative AI and agentic AI in marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/generative-ai-for-marketing): Generative AI creates content in response to prompts. Agentic AI acts on that content, planning and executing multi-step tasks toward a goal with limited supervision. In marketing they pair well: generative AI fuels creation and insight, while agentic AI and decisioning drive orchestration and delivery.

[What are the risks of generative AI in marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/generative-ai-for-marketing): The main risks are hallucinated or inaccurate content, hidden bias, and governance gaps as adoption outpaces oversight. Mitigate them with domain-specific data, mandatory human review and fact-checking, access controls, and a clear governance framework before scaling.

[What is martech consolidation?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/martech-consolidation): Martech consolidation is the process of reducing the number of marketing tools in a stack by replacing overlapping point solutions with fewer, more integrated platforms. The goal is to cut cost and complexity, unify customer data, and make teams faster by removing silos and redundant integrations.

[How do you decide when to consolidate or replace a tool?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/martech-consolidation): Consolidate or replace a tool when it overlaps with others, holds siloed data, is underused, or carries high integration and maintenance cost relative to its value. Start with a stack audit that maps each tool's purpose, usage, cost, and data flows, then target redundancy first.

[What are the signs of martech sprawl?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/martech-consolidation): Signs include overlapping tools doing similar jobs, customer data scattered across disconnected systems, slow campaign execution, heavy engineering time to keep integrations running, low tool adoption, and rising total cost of ownership. When teams stitch tools together to get a single customer view, sprawl is the problem.

[What is the difference between build and buy in martech?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/martech-consolidation): Building means developing custom in-house tooling; buying means adopting a vendor platform. Building offers control but consumes R&D budget and ongoing maintenance; buying delivers faster time to value and vendor-maintained capabilities. Most teams consolidating a stack favor buying integrated platforms over maintaining homegrown point solutions.

[How does a customer engagement platform reduce martech sprawl?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/martech-consolidation): A customer engagement platform consolidates channels (email, push, SMS, in-app, web), a unified customer profile, and decisioning into one system, replacing several point tools. That reduces integration overhead and data silos, so teams orchestrate cross-channel journeys from one place instead of stitching separate tools together.

[What is AI content marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): AI content marketing is the use of artificial intelligence across the content lifecycle, from ideation and drafting to optimization and distribution, to produce and deliver relevant content more efficiently. It typically combines generative writing models, data analysis for topic and performance insight, and workflow automation, with humans guiding strategy and quality.

[Will AI replace content marketers?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): No, AI will not replace content marketers. AI handles data analysis, structuring outlines, and drafting, but it lacks the strategy, empathy, and lived experience that build real audience connection. The strongest teams use AI for the repetitive heavy lifting and keep humans in charge of strategy, voice, and final quality.

[What can AI do across the content marketing lifecycle?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): Across the content marketing lifecycle, AI supports ideation (topic clustering and gap analysis), drafting (outlines and first drafts), optimization (semantic SEO and competitor gaps), and distribution (repurposing one asset into channel-specific formats). Applied to the right stage, it speeds production while humans handle nuance, accuracy, and brand voice.

[What are the risks of AI content marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): The main risks of AI content marketing are over-reliance, producing generic text, brand-voice dilution, hallucinated facts, and missing audience empathy. You can mitigate them with detailed brand-voice constraints, a mandatory human-in-the-loop review, rigorous fact-checking against primary sources, and continuous performance auditing.

[What is the difference between generative AI and AI decisioning in content?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): The difference between generative AI and AI decisioning is what each one does with content. Generative AI creates content, such as drafts, variants, and headlines, while AI decisioning chooses which content each person sees and when, based on observed behavior. Content marketing gets the most value when generation supplies the library and decisioning delivers the right piece to each customer across channels.

[What are the best AI content marketing tools?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-content-marketing): The best AI content marketing tools depend on the job you need done, so it helps to think in categories rather than a single ranking. A well-rounded stack usually covers generative writing for drafts and variants, data analysis and SEO tools for ideation and optimization, workflow automation for approval and scheduling, and AI decisioning to select and deliver the right content to each customer across channels.

[What is Braze Connected Content?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-connected-content): Braze Connected Content is a Liquid-based feature that pulls information from any public or private API into your messages at send time, letting marketers deliver real-time, personalized content without duplicating data into Braze first. It's designed to retrieve content and data from an external source, like a company's back end or a public API, and use that information to dynamically personalize the content of messages, from weather data to product recommendations.

[Does Connected Content support authenticated APIs?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-connected-content): Yes. Braze offers multiple authentication options for Connected Content. Beyond static credentials, Braze also supports dynamic Liquid variables within both the headers and body of a Connected Content tag, which means the response from one Connected Content call can feed into a subsequent call. This is what makes OAuth flows practical: a brand can make one Connected Content request to retrieve an access token, cache that token for a configurable period, and then template it into a subsequent Connected Content call to authorize against the API using that retrieved token.

[What are Braze CDI Segments?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/introducing-braze-cdi-segments-providing-zero-copy-access-to-your-data-warehouse): Cloud Data Ingestion Segments (CDI Segments) are a Braze feature that gives customers zero-copy access to information and insights held in their own data warehouses, directly from within Braze. Instead of moving warehouse data into Braze first, customers can directly query their data warehouse to create new segments, and add a connected source to the Braze dashboard so those CDI Segments can be used to build and tailor audiences duplicating any of the underlying data into the Braze platform.

[What does zero-copy mean?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/introducing-braze-cdi-segments-providing-zero-copy-access-to-your-data-warehouse): Zero-copy means Braze queries the data warehouse directly to build segments rather than copying or replicating the underlying data into Braze first. The data stays in its original location; Braze accesses it in place instead of ingesting a duplicate copy. This matters for a few concrete reasons: Fewer data copies = better privacy posture. Reducing replication supports compliance with data minimization principles and reduces the risk associated with unnecessary duplicate copies. Smaller breach footprint. Storing fewer copies of the data reduces the potential impact of any data breach or leak, since there's less duplicated sensitive information sitting in additional systems. Less architectural complexity. It eliminates the "numerous middleware layers that query and transport data from warehouses to engagement platforms," which otherwise add complexity and can introduce latency.

[Which data warehouses are supported?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/introducing-braze-cdi-segments-providing-zero-copy-access-to-your-data-warehouse): CDI Segments currently support four data warehouses: Snowflake Google BigQuery Databricks Amazon Redshift

[What are Braze Landing Pages?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-landing-pages-how-to-get-started): Braze Landing Pages are configurable, Braze-hosted web pages built with drag-and-drop layouts, Liquid personalization, and mapped form fields to capture data, update user profiles, and trigger retargeting. Despite the name, they're not just a destination; they play a key role in building a connected, end-to-end customer journey, driving engagement across the full customer lifecycle rather than functioning as an isolated web page. By default they're hosted on a Braze domain, but you can connect a custom domain or subdomain in Workplace Settings for a fully branded URL.

[Do you need a developer to build one?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-landing-pages-how-to-get-started): No, the article positions this as a no-code, self-serve process for marketing teams. You build the page in a drag-and-drop editor from the Messaging tab in the Braze dashboard, either from scratch or from a pre-designed template, adding rows, blocks, and form fields, then customizing colors, fonts, and media. Liquid personalization lets you dynamically tailor headlines, greetings, or CTAs based on who's visiting — no separate dev build required. The whole workflow (design → add form fields → preview across mobile/tablet/web → publish) is described as simple to build and easy to customize.

[What can landing page forms update in Braze?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-landing-pages-how-to-get-started): When a user submits a form, their information automatically updates the corresponding fields in their Braze user profile and form fields map directly to standard or custom attributes. Supported field types include email and phone number capture, text inputs, dropdowns, and checkboxes. A couple of important mechanics: If sent via a Braze channel (e.g., a link added through "Add Personalization" in a message composer), the page can be personalized with known customer data, and submissions are captured directly into that existing user's profile. If sent via a non-Braze channel (social, a website banner, an in-store QR code), a submission creates a brand-new user profile. Submissions can also immediately trigger retargeting — for example, filtering on the "Submitted Form" event to move someone into a new campaign or journey, such as sending a confirmation text right after an SMS sign-up. You can also track views, clicks, and form submissions to monitor and optimize performance over time. Best practice: Link a confirmation page to any form-based landing page.

[What are common landing page use cases?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-landing-pages-how-to-get-started): Common landing page examples include: Growing email opt-ins Building an SMS audience Driving memberships and subscriptions Collecting real-time feedback Spotlighting new products and offers Encouraging app downloads

[What is an in-app message?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/in-app-messages-across-platforms): An in-app message (IAM) is a message that appears inside your app or website while someone is actively using it, and it requires no opt-in. Unlike channels that reach people outside the product, an IAM only shows up when someone's already engaged and looking at the screen.

[What's the difference between an in-app message vs. a push notification?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/in-app-messages-across-platforms): The distinction it implies comes down to when and where each reaches a user: Push notification — arrives whether or not someone is using the app, showing up on the lock screen or notification center. It requires explicit opt-in (a user has to grant notification permissions), and its job is to pull someone back into the app. In-app message — only appears while someone is actively using the app or website, and needs no opt-in since it's not a system-level notification — it's just part of the in-session experience. Its job is to guide or engage someone who's already there.

[Which platforms support IAMs?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/in-app-messages-across-platforms): IAMs now extend across four main platform categories: Smartphones and tablets — notifications delivered when users have a mobile or tablet app open, appearing full-screen or as part of the screen. Websites — the web version, known as in-browser messages, reaches people while they're actively browsing a site on mobile or desktop, and is a great way to capture email addresses to support a cross-channel approach. Smart TVs and OTT devices — including Roku, Amazon Fire TV, Android TV, LG TV, Samsung Tizen TV, and Apple TV, where platforms like Braze can collect engagement data on content preferences and streaming habits to power personalized on-screen messages. AR, VR, and spatial computing — including Apple Vision Pro and visionOS, where Braze's Swift iOS SDK enables in-app messaging directly within the device. Braze also extends push notifications and Content Cards into this space.

[When should you use IAMs?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/in-app-messages-across-platforms): On first open, but only for urgent matters. Only urgent messages, like system updates or password change notices, should be shown when someone first opens the app or site. At personalized, behavior-driven moments otherwise. The best-timed IAMs are personalized and responsive at the device level — for example, timing messages to when a specific user typically engages with a specific platform (mornings on mobile, daytime on web, evenings on a connected TV app). Right after a triggering action. Campaigns can be triggered once a user completes a specific action, like prompting a rating after someone finishes a show, or delivering a reward the moment someone joins a loyalty program. With awareness of the rest of the customer journey. IAMs should be sequenced against other channels so users aren't hit with the same campaign at the same time across channels, or asked for the same feedback (like a satisfaction survey) redundantly on a different device.

[How does personalization impact customer loyalty and retention?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/building-long-term-customer-loyalty): Personalization can have a profound impact on behaviors that lead to customer loyalty and retention. McKinsey & Company research has found that personalization leads to higher customer engagement and can boost average revenue by 10-25%. In addition, Braze research cited above finds that personalization capabilities can help brands drive open, click, and conversion rates, which over time can lead to stronger loyalty and retention outcomes.

[Why is customer loyalty essential for sustainable brand growth?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/building-long-term-customer-loyalty): Customer loyalty is essential for sustainable brand growth because it helps brands combat the rising costs of acquiring new customers and reap the benefits of retaining customers over time, including increased cost savings, profits, and customer referrals. As discussed earlier, the cost of acquiring new customers has been increasing over the past decade and, on average, brands actually lose $29 per new customer acquired. At the same time, as cited above, customers acquired via referrals are better for business, thanks to the “referral contagion” phenomenon mentioned above, which increases the likelihood that these customers will make more purchases and generate even more referrals, compared with customers acquired via other channels.

[What are some proven techniques for retaining modern consumers?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/building-long-term-customer-loyalty): In the Braze guide Retention by Design: Your Anti-Churn Playbook, we reveal five tactics marketers can use to drive stronger retention. Here’s an overview of these powerful techniques: Customer onboarding: Teach new users about the value your brand provides—and encourage deeper engagement sooner, bolstering retention—right from the start with more effective onboarding. Priming for permissions: Get customer opt-ins you need to gather customer data and send customer outreach by clearly explaining the benefits of taking these actions. Asking for customer feedback: Find out and address your customers’ top pain points, interests, wants and needs, and sources of delight. 1:1 personalization: Show customers you know what they care about and give them reasons to keep coming back for more by tying your marketing outreach to their individual user behaviors. Experimentation: Embrace testing and learning to identify opportunities to improve your retention over time.

[How can brands customize notification channels to suit user preferences?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Brands that want to send push notifications to Android users are required to create at least one notification channel. Beyond that, companies have the option of creating multiple channels for different purposes. It’s possible to organize messages into channels by almost any criteria. For instance, you could set up one channel for your organization’s transactional push notifications, another for promotional outreach, and a third for activity messages that provide status updates from a user’s social network, such as alerts about the photos their friends are sharing, the comments they’ve added, or the posts they’ve liked. If you wanted, you could create one channel for push notifications that are text only and a second channel for messages that contain images, or you could set up channels associated with multiple email accounts held by a single user or different in-app groups they belong to. After you determine the channels you’d like to set up, you can use a customer engagement platform like Braze to create and manage your Android notification channels or iOS notification groups.

[What is Braze Intelligent Channel and is it related to notification channels?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Part of BrazeAI™, Braze Intelligent Channel is an AI-powered tool that helps marketers automatically send customers campaigns via their preferred messaging channels to improve marketing outcomes. It is able to instantly identify which type of communication—whether that’s email, SMS, push notification, or another popular messaging channel—a given user is most likely to engage with and automatically send that type of campaign. Braze Intelligent Channel is completely separate from notification channels, which are categories of push notifications brands can create for users to opt into or out of based on their preferences.

[What else can brands do to reduce the odds that users will opt out of push notifications (whether partially or completely)?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Use segmentation and personalization to target push notifications to users based on their interests and behaviors, rather than sending one-size-fits-all messages. Take advantage of a variety of messaging channels (such as email, in-app messages, web push, and more) to reach customers, and use push notifications only when the purpose of a message is a good fit for that type of outreach—for instance, for urgent, timely updates. Create a preference center for your app or website, and use in-app messaging to nudge users to self-determine what messaging channels they want to be reached on as well as what topics or types of messages interest them—instead of waiting for them to opt out of push channels they’re uninterested in.

[What are best practices for organizing notifications by priority?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): 1. Understand what importance or priority levels are and how they work: Priority levels, also known as importance levels, are a property of Android notification channels. The priority level you set for your push notifications determines how they’re displayed in users’ notification trays, how fast they’re delivered, whether they’re delivered with a sound or not, and the interruption level of the message. 2. Work with your developer to configure the priority level for each channel you’re setting up in your customer engagement platform. 3. Use the appropriate notification channels priority level based on the type of message you’re sending.

[What is the role of notification channels in reducing app uninstalls and opt-out rates and improving user engagement rates?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Push notifications can be a powerful way to reach customers with urgent messages, but too often they’re misused by brands as part of a spray-and-pray approach to customer engagement. This runs the risk of alienating users and potentially driving them to opt out of future push notifications or even uninstall the app in question. Push notification channels reduce that danger. Instead of users choosing to opt out of receiving all push notifications if they’re unhappy with a message they receive, notification channels give them the option to partially opt out instead, allowing you to continue reaching them with push notifications they’re willing to receive. While no brand wants someone to opt out of push notifications even in part, this more granular approach to push notification permissions reduces the chances that customers opt out completely or even uninstall your app in frustration. By allowing customers to self-determine the types of notifications they receive from you, push notification channels make it more likely that your push outreach is relevant and valuable to the people receiving it. That said, while push notification channels are a step forward, brands would be smart to treat this feature as a safety net, not as their primary method of reducing push opt-outs or uninstalls. Marketers should still be taking steps to make their experience of push notifications positive enough that they won’t want to opt out in the first place by using features like segmentation, personalization, Intelligent Timing, and frequency capping.

[How do notification channels enhance user control over message types?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Push notification channels make it a lot easier for users to stop receiving messages that they’re not interested in without blocking all notifications from a given app. For example, if a user likes getting breaking news alerts from a media app, but dislikes getting notifications promoting stories that aren’t time sensitive, they could click on one of the latter push notifications, opt out of messages from that channel and get exactly the notifications they want. What are the steps to create effective notification channels for mobile apps? Step 1: Categorize the types of notifications you’re going to send your users and organize them by order of priority. Factors to consider include types of users your brand sends push to, their level of engagement, their specific lifecycle stage, and the goals they’re trying to accomplish with your brand. The more nuanced approach you take, the easier it will be for customers to customize their push experience. At a minimum, brands should split their notification types into two categories: promotional push notifications and transactional push notifications. This way, users can still opt into receiving vital transactional messages, while opting out of less essential marketing campaigns. Step 2: Make sure the notification channels you choose align with your overall messaging strategy. In addition to deciding which notification channels to set up, it’s important to consider your broader customer engagement efforts. Consistency across channels and experiences will help to support strong customer retention and engagement rates. Step 3: Use a customer engagement platform like Braze to set up your notification channels and set the priority level. Next, after setting your strategy for organizing your channels and ensuring this supports your larger customer messaging goals, you can use a customer engagement platform like Braze to create and manage your Android notifications channels or iOS notification groups. You’ll need to work with a developer to configure each channel’s priority level.

[Why is audience segmentation important in managing notification channels?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/what-are-notification-channels-anyway): Audience segmentation is an important part of establishing and managing notification channels that helps brands ensure they’re creating options that align to specific groups of customers’ interests, behaviors, and needs. For instance, a travel and hospitality brand may choose to create distinct channels for leisure travelers and business travelers. That way, travelers can opt out of ONLY the channels that aren’t a fit rather than opting out of ALL push notifications altogether. Not doing so could harm engagement rates and the overall health of a brand’s push subscriber list.

[What is Apple Mail Privacy Protection?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/2025-ios-changes-email-marketing): Mail Privacy Protection (MPP) is a feature Apple introduced in iOS 15 (2021) that's still active today. When enabled, it pre-loads a message's remote images, including the invisible tracking pixel marketers use to register an "open," through Apple's own proxy servers rather than letting the sender's server load them directly when the user actually opens the email. The practical effect: every email appears "opened" almost immediately after delivery, regardless of whether the person ever looks at it, and the IP address marketers see is Apple's proxy, not the recipient's actual location or device. This means open rates for any meaningful share of Apple Mail users (iPhone, iPad, Mac Mail, iCloud Mail) have been structurally inflated and untrustworthy for several years now. This isn't a new problem, but it compounds with newer inbox changes.

[How does iOS 26 categorize email?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/2025-ios-changes-email-marketing): Apple's inbox categorization (introduced in iOS 18.2, carried forward and refined into iOS 26) automatically sorts incoming mail into four buckets: Primary: personal messages and time-sensitive information. Transactions: confirmations, receipts, and shipping notices. Updates: news, newsletters, and social media updates. Promotions: coupons and sales emails.

[Are open rates still reliable?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/2025-ios-changes-email-marketing): The net effect is that open rate today conflates at least three different things with no reliable way to separate them out. It's still useful directionally (a sudden open-rate collapse across a whole domain usually does mean something's wrong), but treating it as a precise engagement number, or optimizing subject lines against it, is no longer sound practice.

[What should email marketers measure instead of opens?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/2025-ios-changes-email-marketing): The more reliable signals cluster into a few categories: Click-through rate (CTR) and click-to-open rate — clicks require a real human action and aren't spoofed by MPP the way opens are. CTR is the closest like-for-like replacement for engagement tracking. Conversion metrics — completed purchases, sign-ups, form fills, or whatever the email's actual goal was. This ties email performance to business outcomes rather than a proxy signal. Downstream behavior — website visits, app opens, or in-app actions attributed to an email click, which tell you what happened after the click rather than stopping at "did they look at it." List health signals — unsubscribe rate, spam complaint rate, and bounce rate. These are unaffected by MPP and are also exactly what Google and Yahoo's bulk sender rules key off of, so they double as compliance signals. Deliverability and placement metrics — inbox placement rate (did it land in Primary vs. get buried), and read time / scroll depth where your ESP supports it, since these get at actual attention rather than a pixel fetch.

[What is email authentication?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/email-authentication-overview): Email authentication is a set of technical standards that let a receiving mail server verify that an email actually comes from the domain it claims to come from, rather than being spoofed by a spammer or phisher. It works by publishing DNS records for your sending domain that mailbox providers (Gmail, Outlook, Yahoo, etc.) check automatically when your email arrives. Without it, there's no cryptographic or policy-based way for a receiving server to confirm you are who you say you are — which is exactly what spammers exploit.

[What is the difference between SPF, DKIM, and DMARC?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/email-authentication-overview): SPF (Sender Policy Framework) — A DNS record listing which mail servers/IP addresses are allowed to send email on behalf of your domain. The receiving server checks the sending IP against this list. It's a simple "yes/no, this server is authorized" check, but it breaks easily when email is forwarded, and it doesn't protect the actual "From" address a recipient sees. DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail) — Adds a digital signature to each outgoing email, generated with a private key and verified by the receiver using a public key published in your DNS. This proves the email's content wasn't altered in transit and that it genuinely originated from your domain. It survives forwarding better than SPF since it's tied to the message itself, not the sending IP. DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance) — Sits on top of SPF and DKIM. It tells receiving servers what to do if a message fails SPF and/or DKIM checks (quarantine it, reject it, or do nothing), and it requires "alignment" — the domain in SPF/DKIM must match the visible "From" domain. DMARC also gives you reporting, so you get visibility into who's sending email using your domain (including potential spoofers). Think of it this way: SPF and DKIM are two independent ways of proving legitimacy, and DMARC is the policy layer that says how strictly to enforce those checks and tells you what's happening.

[Is email authentication required?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/email-authentication-overview): Practically, yes for anyone sending marketing or bulk email today. Gmail and Yahoo enforced bulk sender requirements starting in 2024 that made SPF and DKIM effectively mandatory for anyone sending significant volume, and require DMARC (at minimum a policy of "none") for domains sending 5,000+ messages a day to their users. Without these in place, mail is increasingly likely to be filtered to spam or rejected outright rather than reaching the inbox. It's not a universal legal requirement, but it's become a de facto requirement for deliverability at any real sending volume.

[How to check if your domain is authenticated?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/email-authentication-overview): A few practical ways: Send a test email to your own Gmail account, open it, click the three-dot menu → "Show original," and check the headers for SPF: PASS, DKIM: PASS, and DMARC: PASS. Use a DNS lookup tool (like MXToolbox) to check for TXT records: an SPF record starts with v=spf1, a DKIM record lives at a selector-specific subdomain (e.g., default._domainkey.yourdomain.com), and a DMARC record lives at _dmarc.yourdomain.com and starts with v=DMARC1. Check your ESP's dashboard — most platforms (Braze included) show authentication status for your sending domains directly in their settings, since they need these records configured to send on your behalf.

[What is AI email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): AI email marketing is the use of AI to write email content and decide when, how, and to whom each message is sent, based on what customers actually do rather than fixed rules. It covers two things: generative AI that creates the content, and AI-driven optimization that handles timing, audience, and message selection per person.

[How do you use AI in email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): You use AI in email marketing for five main jobs: generating and testing content variants, optimizing send time per individual, segmenting by live behavior, selecting the next best message through decisioning, and protecting deliverability with AI-assisted monitoring. Each one replaces manual setup with a decision pulled from how each customer behaves.

[What is the difference between generative AI and AI optimization in email?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): Generative AI creates the email, drafting subject lines, body copy, and creative variants at volume. AI optimization decides what happens with them, choosing the send time, the audience, and the message each person receives. One produces the range of options. The other selects which option goes to whom.

[What can AI do for email marketing that automation cannot?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): Automation repeats fixed if-then logic a marketer builds once. AI decides from behavior as it happens, so the logic updates itself. It can weigh how each person engaged, choose the message and timing for them individually, and hold a send entirely, rather than treating everyone in a flow the same way.

[Which email platforms have the best AI?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): The best AI depends on what you need. Platforms with built-in AI like Mailchimp, Klaviyo, and Salesforce Marketing Cloud suit single-channel email. Cross-channel platforms like Iterable, MoEngage, and Braze go further, making AI decisions across email, push, SMS, and more from one customer profile.

[What is AI email personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): AI email personalization uses machine learning to tailor email content, offers, subject lines, and send timing to each recipient based on their behavior and preferences. It goes beyond inserting a name, generating and selecting the most relevant content per person and updating as new data arrives.

[How does AI email personalization work?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): It works in two steps: generative AI creates on-brand content variants (subject lines, copy, recommendations), and decisioning models select which variant, offer, and send time each recipient receives based on observed behavior. Machine learning refines those choices continuously as engagement data accumulates.

[How is AI email personalization different from basic personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): Basic personalization inserts known fields like a first name or last purchase. AI email personalization analyzes behavioral and contextual data to tailor content, timing, and offers to the individual, and adapts automatically over time, making messages far more relevant than static merge tags or segment-level rules.

[What data does AI email personalization use?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): It draws on first-party behavioral data (opens, clicks, purchases, browsing), profile attributes, lifecycle stage, and real-time context like location or time. Unified in one customer profile, this data lets models predict intent and choose the most relevant content and send time for each recipient.

[Does AI email personalization replace marketers?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): No. AI handles data analysis, content generation, and per-recipient selection at scale, but marketers set strategy, guardrails, and brand voice and review output for quality. The best results come from AI doing the heavy lifting while humans keep messaging on-brand and on-strategy.

[What is eCommerce email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-email-marketing): eCommerce email marketing is the practice of using email to promote products, recover sales, and build loyalty for an online store. It spans the full journey, from welcome and promotional campaigns to abandoned cart recovery, transactional messages, and post-purchase follow-ups, usually automated and personalized to each shopper.

[Why is email marketing important for eCommerce?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-email-marketing): Email is a direct, owned channel with consistently high ROI that reaches shoppers without algorithm gatekeeping. For eCommerce, it drives acquisition, conversion, and retention, welcoming subscribers, recovering carts, and bringing customers back at a fraction of the cost of acquiring new ones.

[What types of eCommerce emails should you send?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-email-marketing): A balanced program includes welcome emails, transactional and post-purchase messages, abandoned cart recovery, product recommendations, re-engagement and win-back, loyalty and rewards, and promotional or seasonal campaigns. Mixing automated triggers with scheduled campaigns keeps shoppers engaged across every stage of the journey.

[How do abandoned cart emails work?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-email-marketing): Abandoned cart emails re-engage shoppers who added items but did not check out, usually as a timed sequence, starting with a reminder, then a follow-up with an incentive, then a final nudge. Because these shoppers already showed intent, the sequence often recovers real revenue with modest discounting.

[How does AI improve eCommerce email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-email-marketing): AI improves eCommerce email by personalizing content and product recommendations per shopper, optimizing send time individually, and deciding the next best message based on behavior. It also generates content variants at scale, so campaigns stay relevant and efficient as the subscriber base grows.

[What is the difference between Braze and Hightouch?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-vs-hightouch): The difference between Braze and Hightouch is what each one does with your data. Hightouch is a composable CDP that activates warehouse data into other tools, while Braze is a customer engagement platform that unifies data, makes individual-level decisions, and sends messages natively across channels.

[Is Hightouch a customer engagement platform?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-vs-hightouch): No, Hightouch is not a customer engagement platform. Hightouch is a composable CDP and data activation layer that syncs warehouse data into downstream tools, including engagement platforms. It builds and activates audiences, but the messaging is sent by the channels and tools it feeds rather than by Hightouch itself.

[What is a composable CDP and how does it differ from a customer engagement platform?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-vs-hightouch): A composable CDP activates customer data directly from your data warehouse without storing a separate copy. It differs from a customer engagement platform in its job: a composable CDP organizes and syncs data to other tools, while a customer engagement platform like Braze also makes individual-level decisions and executes the messaging.

[Does Braze have a customer data platform?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-vs-hightouch): Yes, Braze includes a data layer through the Braze Data Platform, which ingests and unifies customer data into a single, real-time profile. It differs from a warehouse-native composable CDP because Braze holds that profile in the same platform that handles decisioning and cross-channel execution, though it can also activate data directly from the warehouse.

[When should a team choose Hightouch vs Braze?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/braze-vs-hightouch): A team should choose Hightouch when data already lives in a warehouse and the priority is activating it across an existing stack with engineering support. A team should choose Braze when the priority is cross-channel orchestration, individual-level decisioning, and native execution from one marketer-operated platform. Both platforms can work together too.